  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 42a7ef911f4c60e408989a759d2b9b18, type: 3}
  m_Name: PY_DATA_TYPES
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::CodeEscape.Programming.LessonData
  id: PY_DATA_TYPES
  language: 1
  conceptTitle: Data Types
  symbol: int str
  relatedQuestionIds:
  - PY_02_DATA_TYPES
  topics:
  - Data Types
  definition: A data type tells Python what kind of value something is. The type
    decides what you can do with the value.
  howItWorks: 'The four basic types are: int - whole numbers like 7; float - numbers
    with a decimal point like 2.5; str (string) - text inside quotation marks like
    "hello"; bool - True or False. Quotation marks always make a string, even when
    the text looks like a number.'
  exampleCode: 'age = 12

    height = 1.5

    nickname = "Ace"

    lucky = "7"

    ready
    = False'
  exampleExplanation: 'age is an int and height is a float. nickname is a str. lucky
    is also a str, because "7" is inside quotes: it is the text 7, not the number
    7. ready is a bool.'
  tip: type(age) tells you a value's type - for age it shows <class 'int'>.
